    "id": "prop:marx-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "When the complier outcome support is full and closed in the population support, every coordinate projection of \\(S(q)\\) equals the corresponding uniformly sharp rank-similar counterfactual-average interval in Marx's Proposition 5; for \\(K=2\\), the path representation reduces to the two monotone common-rank paths.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "K",
      "q",
      "S"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:rank-similarity",
      "thm:path-representation",
      "lem:marx-proposition-five"
    ],
    "justification": "The reduction anchors the new representation to the published full-support benchmark.",
    "gap": "The published benchmark does not supply the finite-alphabet joint set or its path decomposition when complier support is incomplete.",
    "consumer": "Applied users recover the familiar Marx intervals whenever the empirical support condition is credible.",
    "proof_tex": "Under the stated full-support condition, the cited Marx proposition applies to precisely the latent-index, exclusion, independence, and existential rank-similarity model used here.  For a fixed policy threshold \\(p^\\ell\\), its counterfactual average is\n\\[\n\\bar y(p^\\ell)=\\mathbb E[\\mathbf 1\\{U\\le p^\\ell\\}Y_1+\\mathbf 1\\{U>p^\\ell\\}Y_0],\n\\]\nwhich is the \\(\\ell\\)-th coordinate of the causal vector defining \\(S(q)\\).  The path-representation theorem proves that \\(S(q)\\), rather than a relaxation, is the set generated by all laws in that same model.  Therefore validity of Marx's two bounds contains the coordinate projection of \\(S(q)\\), and uniform sharpness supplies attainable laws at its two endpoints and all values in the stated sharp interval.  The projection consequently equals Marx's interval.  For \\(K=2\\), any maximal path from \\((1,1)\\) to \\((2,2)\\) has two steps and must be either\n\\[\n(1,1),(2,1),(2,2)\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n(1,1),(1,2),(2,2),\n\\]\nso the allocation representation has exactly the two claimed common-rank paths.",
